  "theorem_name": "ExponentialSelectorExactExceptionCountAndSharpTemperature",
  "declared_proposition": "For nonnegative integer Goldbach counts A_1,...,A_B and 0<q<1, let E(q)=sum q^(A_i) and let Z be the number of zero counts. Then Z<=E(q)<=Z+(B-Z)q. Consequently Bq<1 implies floor(E(q))=Z, so the exponential selector reconstructs the exact number of Goldbach exceptions in a finite block. The universal threshold is sharp: at Bq=1 the all-one vector has Z=0 but E=1.",
  "mathematical_argument": "Every zero count contributes exactly one. Every positive integer count contributes at most q, which proves the two-sided bound. If Bq<1, the positive tail lies in [0,1), and taking the integer floor recovers Z. Conversely, choosing A_i=1 for every i gives E=Bq, so no q with Bq>=1 can make E<1 a universal coverage test. The prime audit computes each finite representation vector exactly; it does not provide an all-block arithmetic upper bound.",
